                <pb facs="0094" n="82" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg82"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/9–1640: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Braden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-09-16T16:00:00-05:00">September 16, 1940—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>273. My telegram No. 270, September 14 [<hi rendition="#italic">15</hi>], 1940.
                    In an hour’s conversation with Minister of War this morning, it was agreed I
                    should prepare for him memorandum of all points we desire covered in staff
                    agreement. Based on this, he will endeavor to draft the latter and will have
                    further consultations with me thereon.</p>
                <p>Two points emphasized by Minister of War were: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Delicate political situation necessitates utmost caution by Colombian
                            Government in these matters.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>What Colombian Government can and will guarantee without reservation
                            is to maintain internal order and that there shall never be an attack on
                            the Canal from its territory.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Braden</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
